Slide 1: Title

“Today I’ll cover Generative AI and AI Agents. People often use the two terms as if they mean the same thing, but they don’t.”
“The idea I’ll use throughout is that Gen AI is the engine and the AI Agent is the driver.”
“By the end you’ll see what each one does, where each is used, and what risks come with them.”
Slide 2: Advancement of AI

“Think of these as nested layers. Each one sits inside the one before it.”
AI (1950s): the early goal was to simulate human intelligence in machines.
Machine learning (1959): instead of hand-written rules, algorithms learn patterns from data.
Deep learning (1986): multi-layered neural networks, loosely modelled on the human brain, handle complex data such as images and language.
Generative AI (2014): models stopped only classifying and began creating new text, images and code.
Agentic AI (2023): systems that plan and carry out tasks independently.
“The point is that we’ve moved from AI that predicts, to AI that creates, to AI that acts.”
Slide 3: Practical applications of Generative AI

“Gen AI is a subset of AI that generates text, images, audio, video and 3D models. It is already used in gaming, entertainment, product design and scientific research.”
Text: chatbots, content writing and language tasks (ChatGPT, GPT-4).
Images: built with techniques like VAEs, GANs and diffusion models (Midjourney, DALL-E).
Video and speech: predicting frames, cloning voices and converting text to speech (Sora, Canva).
Code and data: code suggestions, quick prototyping and synthetic training data (GitHub Copilot, Claude).
“Gen AI produces realistic, complex content that can resemble human creativity, but it still waits for a person to prompt it.”
Slide 4: Technology stack of a Gen AI model

“Behind every Gen AI product there are five layers. I’ll go from the bottom up.”
Infrastructure: cloud capacity, new chip architectures and efficient algorithms. Cost and carbon footprint matter here.
Data: enterprises must manage the full data lifecycle, with new data types and huge volumes.
Foundation models: a maturing market of pre-trained models. Companies balance size, transparency and performance when choosing one.
Fine-tuning: this adapts a model to our needs and takes multidisciplinary skills, such as engineering, psychology, linguistics and library science.
Applications: delivered through cloud APIs and embedded into products. Firms fine-tune with their own proprietary data.
“The takeaway is that Gen AI is a whole stack, not just a model, and it needs investment at every layer.”
Slide 5: AI Agents in action

“An AI agent uses AI models to plan, make decisions and execute multi-step tasks. It works with external tools, software and APIs in a continuous loop until the goal is met.”
“It is already used in customer support, software engineering, finance operations and workflow automation.”
Autonomous coding: fixing bugs, testing and deploying code (Devin, GitHub Copilot).
Customer operations: resolving tickets, looking up orders and processing refunds (Intercom Fin, Salesforce Agentforce).
Workflow automation: tasks across apps, email triage and scheduling (Zapier Central, Copilot Studio).
Deep research: browsing the web, combining data and writing reports (Perplexity, OpenAI Operator).
“In every case the agent finishes the whole task, not just one answer.”
Slide 6: AI agent architecture

“An agent needs a few more building blocks than a plain model.”
Reasoning models: an advanced LLM is the brain. It plans steps, writes code and evaluates outcomes.
Orchestration and frameworks: this layer manages the plan-act-observe loop, routes decisions and coordinates multiple specialized agents.
Tools and memory: connects the agent to external APIs, web browsers and vector databases, so it has real-time context.
Infrastructure and guardrails: secure sandboxes, cloud compute and monitoring layers keep the agent safe, fast and cost-controlled.
Applications: the agent is embedded into workflows and chat interfaces, and handles multi-step tasks across tools on its own.
“The takeaway is that the LLM is just one part. Tools, memory and guardrails make it a working agent.”
Slide 7: Generative AI is the engine, AI Agents are the driver

“This is the central idea of the talk.”
Top flow, Gen AI: a prompt goes in, the model produces an output, and it ends there. It is one step and it needs a human to prompt it.
Bottom flow, AI Agent: you give it a goal. It reasons using the LLM, acts through tools, observes the outcome, and repeats until the goal is met.
“The LLM is the reasoning core in both. The agent wraps a loop and tools around it.”
“An engine can produce power, but only a driver decides where to go and keeps going until arrival.”
Slide 8: From Gen AI to AI Agent

“In short, Gen AI responds to prompts and agents pursue goals. I’ll compare them row by row.”
Reactive vs autonomous: Gen AI answers single prompts with one-off outputs. Agents break broad goals down without constant prompting.
Content-focused vs tool-equipped: Gen AI creates text, images and code in a chat window. Agents connect to apps, browsers and APIs and take real action.
Human-driven vs self-correcting: Gen AI needs manual prompting and review at every step. Agents review their own work, fix errors and adapt.
Session-bound vs persistent memory: Gen AI is limited to the current conversation window. Agents remember context across long, multi-step tasks.
Value: Gen AI gives faster content creation and brainstorming drafts. Agents give end-to-end task completion, not just drafts.
“Agents don’t replace Gen AI. They are built on top of it.”
Slide 9: Challenges in Gen AI and AI Agents

“Both carry risks, and the agent risks are bigger because agents can act.”
Gen AI:
Hallucinations: the model generates plausible text, but facts aren’t guaranteed.
Data leakage: prompts can accidentally expose sensitive company data.
Bias and quality: outputs reflect flaws in the training data and need human editing.
AI Agents:
Compounding errors: in an autonomous loop, one mistake can spread across tools.
Unauthorized actions: with API access, an agent could make unintended changes or payouts.
Runaway loops: unchecked reasoning cycles can spike compute and API costs.
“In short, Gen AI risks are mostly about wrong content, while agent risks are about wrong actions.”
“Mitigations include human review, limited permissions, spending caps and monitoring.”
Slide 10: Closing and discussion

“To summarise: Gen AI is the engine, agents are the driver, and humans stay firmly in control.”
“My recommendation is to start with Gen AI for content tasks, and introduce agents step by step, with guardrails and human approval for any action that matters.”
“Thank you. I’m happy to take questions.”
Likely questions from your manager

“Is an agent just a better chatbot?” No. A chatbot answers, while an agent takes actions through tools and loops until the goal is done.
“Where should we start?” Start with low-risk, high-volume tasks like drafting, summarizing and email triage, and keep a human approving any action.
“What’s the biggest risk?” For Gen AI it’s wrong or leaked information. For agents it’s unauthorized actions and compounding errors, so permissions and monitoring matter most.
